Subject: wasix: hold a file's lock across its flush
Last-Update: 2026-10-01

A file's descriptors share one host handle, a tokio file, behind a lock.
While an operation of the file is in flight only the last task to poll
it is woken when it completes, so every use of the handle holds the
lock from its first poll until it is done - except the flush that close,
fdatasync, fd_renumber and posix_spawn's dup2 made, which took the lock
afresh on each poll. A host write returns before it has finished, and a
flush that finds one under way waits for it: between two of the flush's
polls another thread's write could take the lock, wait on the same
write and take over the wakeup, so that the flush was never polled
again. With threads writing a file, a close or an fdatasync of it hung.
A flush now holds the lock across its whole wait, as every other use
does.

Closing descriptors in bulk, when a process exits or execs, still takes
the lock afresh on each poll: its futures must be Send, and one holding
the lock across the wait would not be. Such a flush can still stall
while another thread or process writes the same file.

